(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A child has breakthrough varicella with lesions that do not form crusts. How is the end of the contagious period determined?","choices":["When 21 days have elapsed since the exposure","When the fever has been absent for 24 hours","When no new lesions have appeared for 24 hours","When the last lesion has been present for 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the rule for breakthrough disease without crusts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on his trunk. They blanch with pressure and have accumulated over the last decade. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyogenic granulomas","Petechiae","Cherry angiomas","Spider angiomas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — acquired, increasing with age, once called senile angiomas. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How is naevus spilus managed?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and total spine at presentation","Observation with periodic clinical evaluation, plus sun protection counselling","Excision with negative margins at the time it is first identified clinically","Cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal if preferred"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What distinguishes solar lentigo from lentigo maligna?","choices":["Solar lentigo appears in early childhood; lentigo maligna appears only after the age of seventy","Solar lentigo appears on covered skin; lentigo maligna on exposed skin","Solar lentigo has uniform pigmentation; lentigo maligna is asymmetric with irregular border and colour","Solar lentigo is raised and rough; lentigo maligna is flat and smooth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and dermoscopy is essential — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for seborrhoeic dermatitis?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","Oral doxycycline","Topical antifungals such as ketoconazole, with steroids used early to reduce inflammation","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone, continued long term as monotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Scalp disease uses ketoconazole or selenium sulfide shampoo; facial disease uses ketoconazole cream or lotion. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How should laboratory tests and imaging be sequenced in suspected necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["They should not delay surgical intervention, because this is a surgical emergency with high mortality","They should be completed and reviewed before surgery, because the findings determine the incision","They should replace surgery where imaging is negative, because operating carries its own risk","They should be repeated at forty-eight hours, because early results are usually uninformative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood count, chemistry, arterial blood gas, urinalysis and cultures are all sent. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with widespread flaccid bullae and painful oral erosions has a biopsy showing acantholysis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, given urgently","Ultrapotent topical steroids alone","Observation, since the disease usually remits in five to six years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pemphigus is life-threatening, so treatment is urgent, and a steroid-sparing agent is usually added so steroids can be tapered. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"What is a telangiectasia?","choices":["An acquired proliferation of capillaries and venules forming a deep red papule under five millimetres","A rapidly growing vascular papule that arises in response to trauma or hormonal change","A permanently dilated capillary under one millimetre, blanchable","A congenital dilation of dermal capillaries through the full depth of the dermis, present at birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an acquired vascular lesion, single or in groups, with a central punctum. It may be primary or secondary and is associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is pyoderma gangrenosum, despite its name?","choices":["A vasculitis of medium-sized vessels with immune complex deposition","A malignant ulcerating tumour of keratinocyte origin","A neutrophilic dermatosis that is not infectious","A deep bacterial infection requiring prolonged antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dysregulated innate immune activation with neutrophil recruitment causes the tissue destruction. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 60-year-old is being examined for a suspicious lesion. Which additional sites does the deck ask you to include, and when is the oral cavity specifically indicated?","choices":["The affected area alone, since the total-body survey, the acral sites and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist","The scalp and trunk only, since acral sites and the oral mucosa are rarely involved in cutaneous malignancy","Palms, soles and nails in all; the oral mucosa specifically when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease","Palms, soles and nails in every patient; the oral mucosa specifically when melanoma is possible; and the regional nodes for every lesion whatever its risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the oral examination is tied to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Sensitivity is specifically related to fewer of which type of erroneous result?","choices":["Fewer false-positives","Fewer true-positives","Fewer true-negatives","Fewer false-negatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states sensitivity is related to fewer false-negatives, and notes explicitly that sensitivity does not address false-positives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman handling her pet tarantula developed a red painful eye an hour later and now has blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid to the eyelid skin with review in one week's time","Oral antihistamine with artificial tears and reassessment in three days","Irrigation of the eye at home with saline and no further follow-up needed","Urgent ophthalmology referral, since embedded hairs can cause corneal granuloma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical corticosteroid addresses only the cutaneous reaction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which intervention is described as the single most evidence-supported for photoaging?","choices":["Chemical peeling with glycolic or trichloroacetic acid","Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen at SPF 30 or above","Ablative laser resurfacing performed every few years","Daily topical vitamin C at 10 to 20% concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, for both preventing and slowing progression, and beginning in childhood gives maximal lifetime benefit. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise behavioural counselling?","choices":["As a task that is best delegated to non-clinical support staff within the practice","As unnecessary when a guideline exists","As effective only for tobacco cessation","As one of the most important skills for helping patients make behavioural changes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states changing behaviours is difficult and that behavioural counselling is one of the most important skills for supporting it. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"That same patient is not yet on antiretroviral therapy. Which is the first priority in his Kaposi sarcoma management?","choices":["Reduce his immunosuppressive medication doses, since dose reduction is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel, since systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin intralesional vinblastine to each plaque, since local palliation is the cornerstone of epidemic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local or systemic cancer treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Who should receive recombinant zoster vaccine, and how many doses?","choices":["One dose for immunocompetent adults aged 65 and over, and one dose for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","Two doses only for adults who have never had chickenpox, and no doses at all for anyone with a history of varicella or of prior zoster","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 19 and over, and none for adults who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over, and two doses for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — standard interval two to six months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A ward sister asks how best to reduce pressure injuries among her immobile patients. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotics for every patient with limited mobility","Silicone gel sheeting applied over every bony prominence","Early debridement of any area of erythema that appears","Prevention, and the deck calls it the best measure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — frequent skin assessment, nutrition assessment, moisture control and skin care, repositioning every two hours, pain management, improved mobility and specialty mattresses such as an air mattress. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old dental hygienist has a painful, swollen, erythematous index fingertip with grouped vesicles. She had burning and tingling in the finger the day before. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Onychomycosis","Herpetic whitlow","Bacterial felon","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a prodrome followed by grouped vesicles on a swollen digit. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"The same patient asks whether removing them will stop new ones appearing. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They will fade on their own within a year","Each one should be biopsied because they are premalignant","New lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them","Removing them prevents further lesions from forming"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary unless they bother him. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Where should a patient with necrotizing fasciitis be admitted?","choices":["An ambulatory unit for daily dressing changes and review","A surgical intensive care unit, ideally a burn or trauma centre","A general medical ward with hourly observations","A dermatology inpatient unit for wound management"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a team approach with consultations is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"What are the four phases of wound healing, in order?","choices":["Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodeling","Inflammation, hemostasis, remodeling, proliferation","Proliferation, hemostasis, inflammation, remodeling","Hemostasis, proliferation, inflammation, remodeling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — abnormal healing is loss of control over this balance. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What determines management of a congenital melanocytic naevus?","choices":["Pigment intensity alone, with darker lesions excised and lighter ones observed","Melanoma risk together with cosmetic and functional considerations, removing as much as possible while preserving function","Lesion size alone, with every lesion over 5 mm excised regardless of its site or of graft availability at all","Patient age alone, with all lesions excised before the child reaches school age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — observation may be the better option where there is little skin for a graft site. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Where does melanoma rank among cancers in the United States, and what is its standing among skin diseases?","choices":["The 4th most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The 4th most common cancer, but a rare cause of death compared with squamous cell carcinoma","The 2nd most common skin cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — incidence doubled over the preceding 30 years. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why does the lecture caution against dismissing vitiligo as cosmetic?","choices":["It always progresses to universal depigmentation if it is left untreated","It affects patients psychologically and socially, through low self-esteem and poor body image","It carries a high risk of transformation into melanoma within the depigmented areas","It indicates an underlying internal malignancy in a substantial minority of patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — psychological intervention is named as part of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which age group is most affected by head lice, and how is it usually transmitted?","choices":["Infants under one year, primarily by contact with the bedding of an infested carer","Children between three and twelve years, primarily by direct head-to-head contact","Adults over forty years, primarily by sexual contact and shared towels or bedding","Homeless adults, primarily by contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Indirect spread through combs, brushes, bedding and headgear is less common. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which systemic therapy has the strongest current evidence in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given for the first week","Prophylactic antibiotics covering skin and gut flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with intravenous immunoglobulin and etanercept as adjunct options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How is scaling described?","choices":["Accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white","A dried crust of serum","A scratch or abrasion in which the top layer of the skin is worn away by repeated rubbing or friction","Thickening and hardening of the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man treated for hookworm folliculitis has persistent follicular pustules after one course of albendazole. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Switch to topical therapy, which is more effective for the follicular form","Excise the affected follicles, since surgery clears resistant lesions","Apply cryotherapy to each pustule, since freezing kills the larvae","Repeat the course, since hookworm folliculitis may need repeated treatments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the folliculitic form is specifically noted to be more resistant. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How is an id reaction treated?","choices":["With a potent topical corticosteroid to the distant site","With oral antifungal directed at the distant site","With oral antihistamines alone until it settles","It resolves when the primary dermatophyte infection is treated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treating the distant eruption itself is not the answer. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has redness, swelling and tenderness around the right index fingernail three days after a manicure, with a small collection of pus visible under the nail fold. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Herpetic whitlow","Felon of the fingertip","Acute paronychia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — onset two to five days after trauma, starting as cellulitis and progressing to abscess. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"What causes herpes zoster, and what raises the risk?","choices":["Reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus; risk rises with age and impaired cell-mediated immunity","A new infection with varicella-zoster virus caught from an infected contact; risk rises with crowding and poor hygiene","Reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in a sensory ganglion; risk rises with ultraviolet exposure and febrile illness","An autoimmune response to a previous varicella infection; risk rises with atopy and connective-tissue disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the virus stays latent in cranial-nerve or dorsal-root ganglia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How is mild perioral dermatitis treated once the corticosteroid is stopped?","choices":["A higher-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily","Topical ketoconazole","Oral prednisone","Topical metronidazole, erythromycin, pimecrolimus or azelaic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with oral tetracycline or doxycycline for more extensive or persistent disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What treatment philosophy does the deck set out for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Immediate aggressive systemic chemotherapy for all stages regardless of extent, because early aggressive treatment reliably cures the disease and prevents progression to systemic involvement","Observation alone at every stage without skin-directed treatment, because no therapy has been shown to alter the course of the disease or to relieve symptoms","Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial therapy for every patient regardless of stage, because skin-directed topical therapy has no demonstrated benefit","A stage-directed, skin-first approach for most early disease, because early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure or prevent progression and may cause complications and premature death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — aggression is a harm here, not a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A fluid-filled lesion greater than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A pustule","A nodule","A bulla","A vesicle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a vesicle is the same lesion up to one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vitiligo has been using a topical corticosteroid on her eyelids for several months. Which risks should be checked for?","choices":["Skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure","Increased skin cancer risk and photosensitivity","Teratogenicity and the need for pregnancy testing","Tooth staining and hepatotoxicity from the agent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a topical calcineurin inhibitor suits areas where steroids cannot be applied. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old kidney transplant recipient, six years post-transplant, has developed his fourth squamous cell carcinoma this year. Which does the deck say about this pattern, and what can be offered to reduce new tumours?","choices":["Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant and are often aggressive; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge within six weeks of transplant and are usually indolent; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new tumours by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; oral acitretin reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; nicotinamide reduces new tumours by about 20%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both the timing and the chemoprevention figure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a biopsy-confirmed pigmented spindle cell naevus of the thigh. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Excision with negative margins","Observation with annual photographs","Cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement","Sun protection counselling alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is benign but excision with clear margins is the described management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has been treated for head lice and her mother asks what to do about the house. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Put clothing and bedding from the past week in the dryer or bag it for two weeks, wash combs and vacuum","Have the home professionally fumigated and then discard all the pillows and the soft furnishings","Wash everything at 60 degrees Celsius and treat every household member immediately","Engage a professional exterminator to treat cracks and crevices around the beds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fumigation is specifically not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"When can cellulitis be managed without any diagnostic workup?","choices":["Only after blood cultures have returned negative and inflammatory markers are normal","Limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs and no risk factor for serious illness","Any case in which the patient is afebrile, regardless of the extent of the affected area","Any case occurring in a patient under fifty years of age with no history of diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serious infections warrant blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"In which patients might scabies excoriations be seen along the head and neck?","choices":["Patients who have already been treated once","Infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised","Adolescents and young adults in shared housing","Pregnant patients in their third trimester"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — healthy adults have head and neck sparing. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 62-year-old attends about a new lesion on his sun-exposed forearm. Beyond addressing the lesion, which counselling does the lecture say to offer?","choices":["Sunscreen, sun avoidance at peak hours, and skin checks","Avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","Repositioning every two hours and barrier creams","Well-fitting footwear and padding inside the shoe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to any new lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a firm, movable, round nodule on his upper back. There is a small central pore, and pressing it expresses cream-coloured pasty material with an unpleasant smell. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatofibroma","Pilonidal cyst","Epidermoid cyst","Lipoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the central punctum and the odour of the keratin contents. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has about forty moles, most 3 to 5 mm, evenly coloured, round and sharply demarcated. He asks whether he has too many. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Numbers are unrelated to melanoma risk, so no monitoring or sun protection is required at all","Numbers this high define dysplastic naevus syndrome, which requires over one hundred by adolescence","Numbers peak in the thirties and then decline, but melanoma risk rises with the number, so sun protection and monitoring matter","Numbers continue to rise throughout life, and any patient with over twenty naevi needs them all excised early"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — light skin tone and a tendency to sunburn increase the number. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with rapidly progressive alopecia areata is distressed and withdrawing socially. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The hair loss is permanent, because the follicle and its stem cells have been destroyed","The condition is contagious to household members","Nail changes exclude the diagnosis","Psychological support is one of the most important considerations, and support groups may be offered"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the most important factors in managing this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"Which three genera account for the majority of dermatophyte infections?","choices":["Microsporum, Malassezia, Epidermophyton","Trichophyton, Aspergillus, Epidermophyton","Microsporum, Trichophyton, Epidermophyton","Candida, Malassezia, Trichophyton"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and infections are then classified by body location. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is a concern in a drained furuncle?","choices":["Defer drainage until the culture result becomes available","Culture the material obtained at incision and drainage","Start linezolid empirically before any material is obtained","Swab the nasal vestibule of every household contact first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and give endocarditis prophylaxis to at-risk patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which class is the typical first-line topical therapy for comedonal and inflammatory acne?","choices":["Topical retinoids, which are comedolytic and normalise hyperkeratinisation","Topical antimicrobials, which act against Cutibacterium acnes in the follicle","Topical dapsone gel, which is anti-inflammatory by an unknown mechanism","Topical azelaic acid, which is antimicrobial, comedolytic and anti-inflammatory"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — adapalene, tazarotene and tretinoin, generally the most effective for mild disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man is considering botulinum toxin for axillary hyperhidrosis. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is effective for three to six months, so retreatment will be needed","It permanently destroys the sweat glands in the treated area","It carries a risk of compensatory sweating elsewhere in over 30%","It commonly causes dry mouth and urinary retention as side effects"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation is specifically named in the patient education. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a drained furuncle growing methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. He asks why the first antibiotic did not work. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["The organism was not reached because the lesion had not yet been drained at all","The antibiotic was taken for too short a period to eradicate any skin infection","The organism produces epidermolytic toxins that inactivate oral antibiotics","The empiric agents cephalexin and dicloxacillin do not cover this resistant organism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the culture is what redirects therapy to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Specificity is related to fewer of which type of erroneous result, and is silent about which other?","choices":["Fewer false-negatives; silent about false-positives","Fewer false-positives; silent about false-negatives","Fewer true-positives; silent about true-negatives","Fewer false-positives; silent about true-positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Specificity is related to fewer false-positives, and the lecture notes that specificity does not address false-negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fever and a generalized itchy eruption. On the trunk, scalp and face there are macules, papules, vesicles and crusts all present at the same time. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Molluscum contagiosum","Herpes simplex","Varicella"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — several stages appearing simultaneously is the defining feature. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What proportion of patients develop a second basal cell carcinoma, and what does that mandate?","choices":["Up to 50%, which makes at least annual full-skin examination mandatory","Up to 5%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Up to 50%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Under 1%, which makes routine surveillance unnecessary after excision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which pair of barriers to adherence does the lecture list?","choices":["Cultural or religious beliefs about treatment, and fear of side effects or mistrust of providers","High self-efficacy and strong family support","Short travel distance and abundant appointment availability","Excess follow-up appointments and over-explanation of the possible side effects of treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both appear on the lecture's list of adherence barriers. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which two clinical clues does the deck give for suspecting cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in a chronic eruption?","choices":["Sharp demarcation with thick silvery scale, and rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid","Annular plaques with progressive central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border","Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss","Itch that is entirely absent even in extensive disease, and complete sparing of the hair follicles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A patient's scabies has not cleared after individual therapy. Which regimen is described?","choices":["A mid-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily for a month","Ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses, with topical permethrin every three days to weekly","A third overnight application of topical permethrin given one week after the second one","Oral albendazole for three days, repeated if there is no response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same regimen used for hyperkeratotic or immunosuppressed disease. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What drives the commonest, insulin-resistant form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes within the affected intertriginous skin","Chronic ultraviolet exposure driving proliferation of the basal layer melanocytes and increased melanin production","Immunoglobulin A deposition in the dermal papillae activating neutrophils","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Malignant, drug-induced and endocrine forms make up the rest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with no obesity develops widespread velvety hyperpigmentation over eight weeks, involving the axillae, the oral mucosa and the palms, which have a thickened rugose appearance. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical retinoids for cosmetic improvement of the affected areas","Reassure her that the change is cosmetic and arrange routine follow-up with lifestyle advice in six months","Urgent evaluation for an underlying malignancy, particularly of the gastrointestinal tract","Begin metformin to reduce insulin resistance and reassess in three months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — rapid onset, extensive involvement, mucosal change and tripe palms indicate malignant acanthosis nigricans. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 78-year-old nursing home resident has widespread thick flaking scale on the hands, feet and trunk with thickened discoloured nails. He does not complain of itch. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ordinary scabies","Chronic plaque psoriasis","Asteatotic eczema of age","Crusted scabies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a massive mite burden, poorly defined patches, often no pruritus, and highly infectious. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has several uniformly brown round macules 3 to 4 mm across, some on the forearms and some on the buttocks, unchanged through the winter. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevi","Blue naevi","Lentigines","Ephelides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas and do not fade. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which exposure most commonly precipitates perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Repeated shaving of the beard area with a blade","Topical corticosteroid applied to the face","Prolonged immersion of the hands in water","Occlusion of the skin under a plaster cast"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — continued exposure perpetuates it, so the steroid must be stopped. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"}]};
